{"id":"d7ee1b0e-58d2-4c40-8f77-6a30fd79ecf3","object":"chat.completion","created":1784958102,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import sys\\n    sys.setrecursionlimit(10000)\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    \\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Validate initial required symbols present\\n    all_symbols = {n['symbol'] for n in nodes}\\n    for s in required_symbols:\\n        if s not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{s}' not found in nodes\\\")\\n    \\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    # Sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove root or required symbol node\\n        if node['parent'] is None or node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any children are retained\\n        has_retained_children = any(c in retained for c in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        # Compute subtree ids\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        subtree = set()\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            subtree.add(cur)\\n            for child in children[cur]:\\n                if child not in subtree:\\n                    stack.append(child)\\n        # Check if after removal required symbols remain\\n        candidate_retained = retained - subtree\\n        candidate_symbols = set(node_map[i]['symbol'] for i in candidate_retained)\\n        if all(s in candidate_symbols for s in required_symbols):\\n            # Perform removal\\n            retained = candidate_retained\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    \\n    # Sort retained ids\\n    retained_ids = sorted(list(retained))\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The payload has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The goal is to remove removable nodes while preserving that all required symbols are still present in the retained tree. We need to return removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. The ordering: process nodes deeper first (higher depth), then by tie-breaking by node ID (stable). For each node, if it is removable, we tentatively remove it if all required symbols remain represented (i.e., after removal, the set of symbols in retained nodes still covers all required symbols). Also, we must not remove nodes that have retained children? Actually the description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This implies we should not remove a node if any of its descendants are retained. So we need to traverse bottom-up, consider removal, and also ensure that if we remove a node, its children are also removed? But the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And also \"Never remove... a node with retained children.\" So we can only remove a node if its children are also removed (or if it has no children). Essentially we need to decide a set of nodes to remove such that the remaining tree is a subtree (connected?) Actually removal means pruning the subtree at that node? The typical Perses algorithm removes a node and replaces it with its children? Or removes the entire subtree? The problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That sounds like we are deleting the node and its subtree? But then \"node with retained children\" suggests we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. So we must remove a node only if we also remove all its descendants. So the algorithm: process nodes in bottom-up order (descending depth, then by ID). For each node that is removable, check if all symbols that would remain after removing the entire subtree rooted at that node still include all required symbols. Also check that the node is not root and not required symbol. If conditions satisfied, mark for removal. But careful: if a node is removed, its descendants are also removed. So when checking later nodes (which are shallower), we need to consider that some deeper nodes might already be removed. So we should simulate removal: we compute the set of retained nodes initially all nodes. Then we iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then by node ID ascending? Tie break: node ID as stable? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for same depth, sort by node ID ascending. For each node in that order, if it is still retained, removable, not root, and not required symbol (i.e., its symbol not in required_symbols?), and if removing it (and all its descendants) still leaves all required symbols present in the retained set (after removal), then remove it. But we need to ensure that we don't remove a node that has retained children. But if we process bottom-up, children are processed before parents, so if a child is removed, then later parent can be considered for removal. However, after removing a child, the parent might still have other retained children. The condition \"node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. So we need to check that after the child removal decisions, the node has no retained children. In our bottom-up pass, when we consider a node, its children have already been decided (either removed or retained). So we can check if any child is still retained. If none, then we can consider removal.\n\nAdditionally, we need to ensure that required symbols are preserved. Required symbols are symbols that must appear in the final tree. So after removal, the set of symbols of retained nodes must include all required symbols. But note: the root node's symbol is 'root', and required_symbols includes 'root' and others. So we need to check that after removing the subtree, the remaining nodes (excluding that subtree) still collectively contain all required symbols. Since we only remove if the node is not a required symbol itself, and its removal would not remove the only occurrence of some required symbol.\n\nWe'll implement as described.\n\nOutput: removed_node_ids (ordered as we removed them) and retained_node_ids (sorted? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in removal order (the order we process and remove). retained_node_ids should be sorted? Probably alphabetically or by ID. The description says \"sorted retained IDs\", so we sort the retained IDs (ascending string sort). Also \"actual removal order\" - the order in which nodes are removed.\n\nWe'll return a dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nEdge cases: If case is infeasible (e.g., some required symbol not present initially), raise ValueError? The prompt says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should check that all required symbols are present in the initial node symbols. Also check that root is present, etc.\n\nNow write Python code.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Build a dict of nodes keyed by id.\n2. Validate that all required_symbols appear among node symbols.\n3. Build children lists for each node.\n4. Determine initial set of retained node ids (all nodes).\n5. Process nodes in order: sort by depth descending, then by node id ascending.\n6. For each node in that order:\n   - If node id not in retained_ids: skip (already removed).\n   - If node is root (parent null) or node.symbol in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove).\n   - Check if node is removable (field \"removable\" is True). If not, skip.\n   - Check if node has any children that are still retained. If yes, skip (cannot remove because has retained children).\n   - Compute if after removal, all required symbols still remain in retained set. Implementation: compute the set of symbols in retained nodes except for the subtree rooted at this node. So we need to collect all node ids in the subtree. We can do a DFS from node to get all descendant ids. Then candidate retained set = current retained set minus subtree ids. Then check if the symbols of candidate retained set include all required symbols. If yes, then remove: remove node and its subtree from retained set, and append node id to removed list.\n7. After processing all nodes, sort retained_ids (convert to list and sort).\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}.\n\nNote: The removal order is the order we push onto removed list, which is the order we process and decide to remove. That matches \"actual removal order\".\n\nNow code careful: We need to avoid mutating input. We'll copy necessary data.\n\nAlso ensure we handle large inputs? But using recursion for subtree collection might hit recursion depth; but tree depth small (3 in examples). We'll use iterative.\n\nWrite solve(case) function.\n\nNow produce JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the Python source as a string.\n\nWe'll output exactly one JSON object.\n\nLet's write code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2189,"completion_tokens":2079,"total_tokens":4268,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2176},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1521},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2176,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":13},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}